{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Second gate run: BLOCK on one finding, and it is the sharpest one yet\n\nVerdict BLOCK, **1 blocking finding at validity 1.0**, down from 3. The three earlier findings are\nresolved. This one is better than the ones it replaced:\n\n> AC5's committed mechanism ('a reserved tail budget that no earlier section can spend') is named\n> but its ENFORCEMENT in a non-preemptive, sequential, single-process shell script is unspecified.\n> In straight-line bash, if an earlier timeout-wrapped section blocks for its full ceiling, the\n> tail section never starts — **no budget 'reservation' can preempt an already-running blocked\n> call.**\n\nCorrect, and it is a gap in the plan rather than in the code. \"A reserved tail budget\" describes a\n*guarantee*, and in a single-process shell script no mechanism delivers it as stated: once\n`timeout 120 du` is blocked, nothing reclaims that budget for a later section. A reader given only\nthe plan would be entitled to conclude the mechanism is impossible — and on the plan's own wording\nthey would be right.\n\nThe implementation was never doing the impossible thing. It does the **inverse**, which is\nenforceable: rather than reserving budget for the tail, it *shrinks every call's ceiling as the\ndeadline approaches*, so no call can run into the reserved window at all. The gate's own suggested\nfix describes it almost exactly —\n\n> at start-of-run the probe computes an absolute deadline = start + (TimeoutStartSec -\n> TAIL_RESERVE); every section, before beginning, compares `now` to (deadline) and if it cannot\n> finish within remaining budget it skips…\n\n— which is `PROBE_STARTED_AT` + `probe_budget_left` + `clamped` in change 2684. So this is a **plan\ndefect, not an implementation defect**: the description asserted an outcome and omitted the\nmechanism that makes it achievable, and the gate refused to certify a guarantee it could not see a\npath to. That is the correct call.\n\n**AC5 rewritten** to state the enforcement path concretely — the absolute deadline, the\n`min(own ceiling, budget_left)` grant, the derivation that a call starting at any t cannot end\nafter `deadline - reserve` and that no sequence of them can either, and the 1s floor with its\nreason (\"could not be measured\" is publishable; being SIGKILLed is not). Split into separate\ncriteria per the P6 advisory, each stating how it is verified — notably that the GRANT is\nasserted, not merely that the run completed.\n\n### The admission cap is a real trap\n\nThe rewritten description came out at **8,596 chars against the 8,000 admission cap**, which would\nhave blocked the very re-review the gate demands as the remedy. Trimmed narrative — not evidence;\nevery measurement, line number and timestamp is retained — to **7,995**, applied with no\n`description_warning`. Worth stating plainly for the next person: the remedy for a BLOCK is usually\nto say MORE, and the cap pushes the other way, so budget for it.\n\nFourth run started."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788648366850084001,"uuid":"c3566f93-6ae0-4eba-a344-77ac088e0b03"}